      "id": "def-rare-event-efficiency-criteria",
      "concern": "The Definition says, “Logarithmic efficiency is a weaker asymptotic property than bounded relative error,” but its bounded-relative-error definition permits signed unbiased estimators, whereas logarithmic efficiency explicitly requires nonnegativity. For pλ → 0, let R take ±1 with equal probability and set p̂λ = pλ(1 + 2R). Then E[p̂λ] = pλ and RE(p̂λ) = 2, so bounded relative error holds, but p̂λ is negative with probability 1/2 and therefore fails the stated logarithmic-efficiency definition. The comparison needs a nonnegativity qualification.",
